A declarator whose name is a destructuring pattern prints the pattern as written, on one line, after the declaration's keywords, and never its value, even a function (`load`, the arrow function). A pattern wrapped over several lines joins onto one line, and a default value inside the pattern is part of the pattern, so it prints. A declaration with several declarators prints one line per declarator.
== args
input/destructuring.ts
== file input/destructuring.ts
export const { a, b } = load()
const [first, ...rest] = process.argv
const plain = 1, { c } = obj
let {
  width,
  height,
} = size
var { mode = defaultMode() } = options
const { handler } = () => 1
